# Example AmberEdit configuration.
#
# Copy it to ./amberedit.cfg or ~/.ambereditrc and fix up the paths — those are
# where AmberEdit looks when it is started without -c, after $AMBEREDIT_CONFIG.
#
# A line is a key and the values after it, separated by spaces. A value that has
# spaces of its own — or a "#" in it — is written in double quotes; a "#"
# starting a word begins a comment. Keys may be written in any case, and every
# key but "aka", "akamatch", "nodelist", "echolist", "address_macro", "twit" and
# "twit_subj" is written at most once.
#
# Four settings — "origin", "tearline", "twit" and "twit_subj" — may keep their
# values in a file instead of on the line, written "@file:<name>". See "values
# kept in a file" below; everywhere else "@file:" is the text it looks like.
#
# Each setting below says what it does, what it takes and what it defaults to.

# --- who you are --------------------------------------------------------------

# The name a message you write is from. Required; may be set per area in a group
# block. It is also the key JAM finds your lastread record by.
name Vasya Pupkin

# The address a message you write is from, 3D or 4D. Required; may be set per
# area in a group block.
address 2:382/9999.1

# Your other addresses. Optional; one line per AKA.
#aka 2:5020/999.1
#aka 192:168/2

# Which of your addresses netmail to which destinations is written from.
# Optional — a system with one address needs none of it. One line per AKA: the
# AKA first, then the FTN address patterns it answers for.
#
# In a pattern any component may be a "*", and a "*" at the end of it stands for
# that component and every one after it:
#
#   192:*         the whole of zone 192
#   172:16/*      zone 172, net 16, any node and point
#   2:5020/999.*  node 2:5020/999 and all its points, the boss included
#   2:5020/999    that node alone, its points not being it
#   *             anything at all
#
# 5D addresses are not supported, a "@domain" in a pattern is refused. Where the
# patterns of two AKAs both fit an address, the one that pins down more of it
# wins — counting components from the left up to the first "*" — and between two
# that say as much, the line written first. An AKA named by "aka" alone is one
# you have and one that is never picked on its own.
#akamatch 192:168/2 192:* 172:16/*        # 192:168/2 for zone 192, and 172:16/*
#akamatch 2:5020/999.1 2:5020/999.*       # 2:5020/999.1 for that node and points

# A short word typed where a netmail recipient goes, standing for the whole of
# that recipient. Optional; one line per macro.
#
#   address_macro <macro>,<name>,<address>[,"subject"][,attributes]
#
# Type the macro into the "To" name field of a netmail and press Enter: the name,
# the address and — where the line names them — the subject and the attributes
# are filled in, and the nodelist is not consulted. The commas separate the
# fields, blanks around each one mean nothing, and an empty field was not stated:
# "af,AreaFix,2:382/736,,k/s" names the attributes and leaves the subject alone.
# The macro is matched whole and without regard to case, so "af" never fires on
# "Olaf".
#
# The attributes are the short forms AmberEdit shows a message's attributes in —
# Pvt, Cra, Hld, Dir, Imm, Att, Frq, Trs, Orp, K/s, Loc, Rrq, Cpt, Arq, Urq, Rcv,
# Snt, Scn — written in any case and separated by blanks, a slash being unable to
# separate what "K/s" has one inside of. They are ADDED to what the message
# already carries (Loc and Pvt, for a netmail of your own).
#address_macro af,AreaFix,2:382/736,"PASSWORD",k/s
#address_macro ff,FileFix,2:382/736,"PASSWORD"
#address_macro boss,Sysop,2:5020/999

# --- tosser and message bases -------------------------------------------------

# Path to the tosser config. Required unless you declare every area yourself in
# the "area ... endarea" blocks below.
tosser_config ~/ftn/etc/areas

# Format of that config. Required as soon as tosser_config names one — AmberEdit
# does not guess it from the file.
#   "fidoconfig" - hpt-style, EchoArea / NetmailArea / ... lines
#   "areas.bbs"  - line-based areas.bbs
#   "squish.cfg" - Squish's own config, with its attached -$ options
tosser_config_format fidoconfig

# An area of your own, in the fields a tosser config states one with. The blocks
# are ADDED to what the tosser config declares — a local base for your own notes
# is no business of the tosser's — and they are the whole area list where there
# is no tosser_config at all. A tag declared here AND by the tosser stops
# AmberEdit at startup: two answers to "where is the base for this tag" is not
# something to pick a winner for.
#
# The tag stands on the "area" line. Everything else is optional except the
# path, which only a passthrough area does without:
#   path         the base without its extension, as the tosser config writes it
#   type         squish | jam | msg | passthrough. Left out, AmberEdit works it
#                out from the files on disk — but then it cannot CREATE the base
#                for you when you first enter the area, having no format to
#                create it in. State it for a base that is not there yet.
#   kind         echo | netmail | local | bad | dupe. "echo" if left out.
#   description  what the area list's "d" column shows
#   group_label  what its "g" column shows — fidoconfig's -g. Not the same thing
#                as a "group ... endgroup" block, which is settings for areas.
#   address      the AKA the area is presented under. Your "address" if left out
#   link         the downlinks, several to a line and several lines
#
# How an area is READ — charset, origin, template — is a group block's to say,
# and a group covers an area declared here exactly as it covers any other.
#area NOTES
#    path ~/ftn/msg/notes
#    type jam
#    kind local
#    description "Notes to myself"
#endarea

# Character set a message you READ is decoded from when it carries no CHRS
# kludge, or when its kludge names no particular one — as the obsolete
# "CHRS: IBMPC 2" does. Required; may be set per area in a group block.
default_charset CP866

# Character set a message you WRITE is encoded in, and that its own CHRS kludge
# announces. Required; may be set per area in a group block.
compose_charset CP866

# Whether a reply is written in the charset the message it answers was written
# in, rather than in compose_charset. Optional; off by default, and may be set
# per area in a group block.
#
# Where the reply goes into an area other than the one it was read in, it is the
# area it GOES INTO that this is read from, as compose_charset itself is: what a
# message may be written in is the business of the echo it lands in. A forward is
# not a reply and is always written in compose_charset.
reply_original_charset off

# Whether the From, To and Subject of a message you write are cut to the room
# FTS-0001 keeps for them: 35 bytes for either name and 71 for the subject.
# Optional; on by default.
#
# The bytes are counted once the message is encoded — a name of 35 Cyrillic
# letters is 35 bytes in CP866 and 70 in UTF-8 — and the cut falls between
# characters. Off, the fields reach the base as you typed them and the format
# does what it does with them.
compose_fts1_field_limits on

# Which lastread slot in a message base is yours. Optional; 0 by default, which
# is right on a single-user system. Squish (.sql) and Fido *.msg (the "lastread"
# file in the area directory) are arrays indexed by this number; JAM (.jlr) finds
# your record by the CRC of your name and keeps this number in its UserID field.
# If another reader works on the same bases, use the number it does — GoldED
# calls it SQUISHUSERNO / FIDOUSERNO.
lastread_user 0

# --- nodelists & pointlists ---------------------------------------------------

# The nodelists you keep. Optional; one line per nodelist, and their ORDER IS
# THEIR PRECEDENCE — where two of them list one address, the one named first
# keeps it. Nodelists and pointlists both: a "Boss" line is what makes a
# pointlist one, and no setting says which is which. Three ways of naming one:
#
#   ~/ftn/nodelist/nodelist.ndl   a file, by its name
#   ~/ftn/nodelist/NODELIST.ZIP   a zipped nodelist
#   ~/ftn/nodelist/Z2DAILY.999    the newest of the files whose extension is a
#                                 day number, 001 to 366 ("999" is not a day of
#                                 any year, which is what makes it the pattern)
#   ~/ftn/nodelist/Z2PNT.Z99      the newest of the zip archives whose extension
#                                 is Z (or z) and two digits, unpacked into the
#                                 temporary directory (see tmpdir, below) and
#                                 taken away again after reading
#
# The last two may be written as wildcards instead, which some find easier to
# remember than which sentinel means which: "Z2DAILY.*" is "Z2DAILY.999" and
# "Z2PNT.z*" is "Z2PNT.Z99". The name in front of the extension is a glob in
# every case ("z2*.999"), and a wildcard against an extension that names neither
# pattern is simply a glob over the whole filename ("nodelist.n*"), the newest
# file it matches being the nodelist. Only the filename: a "*" in a directory
# stands for itself.
#
# "The newest" is the modification time, with the higher number breaking a tie.
#
# After the path, optionally, the character set that nodelist is written in —
# for example, UTF-8. A line that states none is read in the charset your locale
# names.
#
# AmberEdit compiles them itself at startup whenever they have changed, so a
# nodelist that arrives while it is not running is compiled the next time it is;
# "amberedit --compile" compiles them anyway. A nodelist that is missing or will
# not read is a warning on the way past and never more than that.
#nodelist ~/ftn/nodelist/nodelist.ndl
#nodelist ~/ftn/nodelist/Z2DAILY.999
#nodelist ~/ftn/nodelist/Z2PNT.Z99

# Where the compiled nodelist goes. Required as soon as there is a "nodelist"
# line; on its own it is a compiled nodelist something else keeps up to date.
# Ctrl-N (or F10) in the reader searches it, by an address whole or in part or
# by any part of a sysop's name.
#nodelist_db ~/ftn/nodelist/amberndl.db

# A directory to work in — unpacking a zipped nodelist or echolist, and the file
# a message is handed over in where external_editor names one.
# Optional: without it an "amberedit" directory of your own under the system's
# temporary one is used ($TMPDIR or /tmp), made the first time something needs
# it. Name one here to work somewhere else — a filesystem with room on it,
# or one a reboot does not empty. Nothing is left in it either way.
#tmpdir ~/ftn/tmp

# --- echolists ----------------------------------------------------------------

# The echolists you keep, for the descriptions they carry: what an echo is, in
# the words the network agreed rather than the words your tosser config happens
# to have. Optional; one line per echolist, and their ORDER IS THEIR PRECEDENCE —
# where two of them describe one echo, the one named first keeps it. A line names
# either an echolist or a zip archive of them:
#
#   ~/ftn/echolist/echo50.lst      an echolist, by its name
#   ~/ftn/echolist/elst2601.zip    an archive, unpacked without paths into the
#                                  temporary directory (see tmpdir, above) and
#                                  taken away again after reading. Every ".lst"
#                                  and ".na" in it is read, in the order their
#                                  names sort in, and nothing else in it is
#                                  written to disk at all
#   ~/ftn/echolist/echo*.zip       the newest file whose name matches — which is
#                                  what to write when the echolist you are sent
#                                  carries its month in its name, so that next
#                                  month's is picked up without editing this file
#
# A "*" stands for any run of characters and a "?" for exactly one, in the
# filename only: a "*" in a directory stands for itself. "The newest" is the
# modification time, and where two files share one the later name wins — so a
# name carrying a date settles it in the order such names sort in. Whether what
# was found is an archive is its own name's to say, so "echo*.*" may land on
# either kind and reads whichever it landed on.
#
# Two shapes, told apart by the extension. A ".na" is the areas-to-request list a
# hub publishes — the echotag, some blanks, and the description running to the
# end of the line. Anything else is read as the comma-separated list an echolist
# is usually published as:
#
#   [Status],Tag,Comment,Moderator's Name,Address,
#
# of which the tag and the comment are what AmberEdit reads. Both shapes take ";"
# at the start of a line as a comment. The format has no quoting, so a comma in a
# description is where that description ends.
#
# After the path, optionally, the character set that echolist is written in —
# for example, CP866 for a Russian one. line that states none is read in
# the charset your locale names.
#
# AmberEdit compiles them itself at startup whenever they have changed, exactly
# as it does the nodelists; "amberedit --compile" compiles them anyway. An
# echolist that is missing or will not read is a warning on the way past and
# never more than that.
#echolist ~/ftn/echolist/echo50.lst CP866
#echolist ~/ftn/echolist/elst2601.zip

# Where the compiled echolist goes. Required as soon as there is an "echolist"
# line; on its own it is a compiled echolist something else keeps up to date.
#echolist_db ~/ftn/amberecho.db

# Whether the reader says where the message was written: the sender's location as
# the nodelist gives it, in the rule that closes the header block. Optional; on
# by default, and it costs no row. A sender no compiled nodelist holds leaves the
# rule as it was; a point no pointlist here lists is placed where its boss is.
show_location on

# Whether the reader says how much message there is: its size at the left end of
# the rule that closes the header block — a bare count of bytes under a kilobyte
# ("917") and kilobytes or megabytes above it ("4k"). Optional; off by default,
# and it costs no row when it is on. What is counted is the whole message,
# service lines included, as it stands here in UTF-8 rather than in the bytes the
# base keeps it in.
reader_show_message_size off

# --- diagnostics --------------------------------------------------------------

# A file to write errors to. Optional; without one nothing is written down
# anywhere, which is the default.
#
# Once the screen is up there is nowhere on it to report anything: there is no
# status line, a frame that will not draw says "error: ..." in place of the
# screen, and a key that fails leaves everything as it was and says nothing at
# all. Name a file here and both are kept instead — the time, the screen that
# was up, the key that was pressed, and what went wrong:
#
#   2026-01-30 21:14:05 reader: F9: ~/ftn/msg/linux.sqd: unexpected end of file
#
# One error is one line and each is added to the end of the file, so the log
# outlives a restart and can be watched with "tail -f" while AmberEdit runs.
# Nothing rotates it and nothing empties it: it is written to only when
# something has gone wrong, and a file that will not open is passed over in
# silence — there is nowhere to complain to about the place complaints go. What
# goes wrong before the screen opens is not in here; that is printed to the
# terminal, which is still there to print to.
#error_log ~/ftn/log/amberedit.log

# --- the keyboard -------------------------------------------------------------

# A keyboard layout. Optional; without one AmberEdit uses its own, which is what
# README's key tables describe and what amberkeys.cfg.example holds written out.
#
# The file is a key and a command per line — "l reader.list", "F2 reader.change".
# Moving about is not bindable; naming one of those keys is an error rather than
# a line quietly dropped.
#keys ~/ftn/etc/amberkeys.cfg

# What that file does to the layout AmberEdit already has. Optional; "merge" by
# default. Says nothing without a "keys" line.
#   merge   the file is read on top of the standard layout: a command it does
#           not name keeps the key it has, and a key it gives to something else
#           is taken off whatever had it — a clash is settled for the file. Two
#           screens go on sharing a key, as F2 does between the reader and the
#           editor, so a line moving one of those two leaves the other alone.
#           A file of three lines then says what three keys do and nothing more.
#   clear   the standard layout is thrown away first and the file is the layout
#           ENTIRE: a command it does not name has no key at all. Copy
#           amberkeys.cfg.example — the defaults written out — and edit that,
#           rather than starting from an empty file and finding half the
#           program unreachable.
#keys_mode merge

# --- area list ----------------------------------------------------------------

# How the area list is sorted. Optional; "te" by default. A set of letters,
# written in any case:
#   a   the address (AKA) the area is presented under
#   e   the echoid (the area tag)
#   g   the group, where the tosser config has one
#   t   the type, in the order net, echo, local
#   u   unread messages (try it!)
# Each letter may be preceded by "-" for descending or "+" for ascending (the
# default). The first letter decides and the rest break its ties, so "-u+e" is
# unread descending and then echoid ascending; spaces between letters mean
# nothing. Areas the letters leave equal stay in the order the tosser config
# names them, and arealist_sort "" leaves the whole list in that order. The
# unread counts are those of the moment the list was built.
arealist_sort te

# What a row of the area list holds. Optional; "e c u\nd n" "e d c un" by
# default. One or two formats: with two, the first is the row in a window
# narrower than adaptive_ui_threshold and the second the row in one at it or
# wider, so a wide window can afford the description beside the name where a
# narrow one has to put it underneath; with one, that format stands whatever the
# window is. Each format is a set of letters, written in any case, each
# optionally followed by the width in columns it is given:
#   a   the area's number in the list, counted from one      4 columns
#   e   the echoid (the area tag)                            what is left
#   d   the description (-d in fidoconfig, or an echolist)   what is left
#   g   the group, where the tosser config has one           5 columns
#   c   how many messages the area holds                     4 columns
#   u   how many of them are unread                          4 columns
#   n   a star where anything is unread                      1 column
# A \n — a backslash and an n, this file knowing no escapes of its own — begins
# the next line of the row, and a row may stand up to five lines tall. Every
# line has to hold something: a row that is to have a blank line in it writes
# that line as a space, where an empty one is a \n typed once too often and is
# refused. A width of 0 means "whatever the fixed-width fields leave on this
# line", shared equally where several fields on the line ask for it — each line
# is measured on its own, so the name on a line of its own has the whole width
# whatever stands under it. A space is a space and the only way to a gap between
# two columns, so a format that is to have one is written in quotes — which is
# what tells one format from two: "a e0 c3 u3n" numbers the areas, gives the
# name what the rest leave, and closes with two three-column counts and the star
# hanging off the unread one. A count too wide for its column is shortened
# rather than cut (17342 as 17k, 1034556 as 1M, and a bare + where not even that
# fits). There is one heading row however tall a row is, and it follows the
# format's first line; areas.bbs has no groups, so there a g column stays blank.
# Where the two formats are not the same height, dragging the window across
# adaptive_ui_threshold keeps the selected area where it stands on the screen
# and counts the areas above and below it again.
arealist_format "e c u\nd n" "e d c un"

# Which description an area is shown by where it has two — one from the tosser
# config (or an "area ... endarea" block here) and one from an echolist.
# Optional; "area" by default, "echolist" for the other way round. Only a
# description that says something counts either way: whichever you prefer, an
# empty one steps aside for the other, so an echo your tosser config is silent
# about is described by the echolist in both settings. Where the description is
# shown is the area list's "d" column and the @CDESC/@ODESC template tokens.
arealist_description_priority area

# What the area list's description column shows for an area nothing describes —
# neither the tosser config nor an echolist. Optional; "no description" by
# default, so that the column reads as a missing description rather than an
# unfinished row. Left commented out here because it is the one default that is
# a word: unset, it is said in whatever language the interface is in, and a line
# written here is that line in every language.
# Written empty (arealist_description_default "") the column stays blank, which
# is what it did before this setting existed. The description column is drawn in
# the theme's "dimmed" color throughout, quiet like a kludge line — what the area
# says about itself as much as what stands in where it says nothing: it is the
# one column that is prose rather than a fact about the area, and the names and
# the counts beside it are what the list is read down. Only the column is
# concerned: the @CDESC/@ODESC template tokens are still empty where there is no
# description.
#arealist_description_default "no description"

# Whether the area list draws the reader's scrollbar in its rightmost column.
# Optional; off by default. It is drawn only where the list is longer than the
# screen, and where it is drawn the rows have a column less to lay out in.
arealist_scrollbar off

# Whether the area list opens showing only the areas with something unread in
# them. Optional; off by default, the list holding every area the tosser config
# declares. Ctrl-U moves between the two whenever the list is up, so this says
# where the screen starts and no more — the answer is not written back here.
# An area with no unread messages is off the list whichever way it got that way,
# so reading one to its end takes it off as the reader is left; an area that
# cannot be read is off it too, having no unread messages to show. With nothing
# unread anywhere the list says so in the middle of the screen, and Ctrl-U
# brings the areas back.
arealist_unread_only off

# Where the cursor stands when the program starts: on the first area with
# something unread in it rather than on the first area of the list. Optional;
# off by default, the cursor starting at the top.
arealist_first_unread off

# --- message list -------------------------------------------------------------

# What a row of the message list holds. Optional;
# "amf0 t0 d(%d %b %y)\ns" "amf t s d(%d %b %y %H:%M)" by default. Written
# exactly as arealist_format is — one or two formats, the narrow window's and the
# wide one's, each a set of letters in any case, each optionally followed by the
# width in columns it is given:
#   a   the message's number in the area, counted from one   the numbers in it
#   m   a "*" where you have marked the message              1 column
#   f   who the message is from                              20 columns
#   t   who it is to                                         20 columns
#   s   what it is about                                     what is left
#   d   when it was written                                  what the stamps need
# A \n begins the next line of the row, a row stands up to five lines tall,
# and every line has to hold something — a blank line in a row is written as a
# space. A width of 0 means "whatever the rest of this line leaves", shared
# equally where several fields on the line ask for it, and each line is measured
# on its own.
#
# The two columns with no plain number of their own take a pass each, before the
# fields written 0 are given what is left:
#   "a" stands as wide as the highest number that can go in it — four columns
#       for an area of 1200 messages, six for one of 150402 — and never under
#       three, so that a handful of messages still reads as a column rather than
#       as a stray digit against the left edge.
#   "d" takes what the stamps on the screen come to and no more. A stamp too
#       wide for its column is cut at the spaces rather than mid-word — "15 Aug
#       26 20:28 +0200" to "15 Aug 26 20:28" to "15 Aug 26" to "15 Aug" to "15" —
#       so a column of fourteen shows nine of "30 Jul 26" and would stand five
#       empty; what it does not use goes to the fields written 0 instead. Two
#       stamp columns on one line are measured apart and share the room between
#       them.
# Either may be given a width like any other field: a4 pins the number column,
# d0 hands the stamp whatever the line leaves over.
#
# "d" is the one field written by a format of its own, in brackets after the
# letter and after its width where one is written: d(%d %b %y),
# d10(%Y-%m-%d). It is a strftime format, read exactly as
# reader_datetime_format is and refused on the same terms; everything up to the
# first ")" belongs to it, spaces and all, which is one more reason the whole
# value is in quotes. A "d" with no brackets is reader_datetime_format's, as it
# has always been. Inside the brackets a \n is not the next line of the row —
# it goes to strftime as it was written — and a format wanting a literal ")" is
# not a thing that can be written here.
#
# "m" holds one character or none: a star where the message is one of the ones
# you have marked — with "t" in the list or in the reader, or from the box the
# reader's "s" opens — and a blank the rest of the time. It stands in the default
# formats where the blank between the number and the first name used to, so a
# list with nothing marked in it reads exactly as it always has; and the column
# is reserved whether or not anything is marked, a field that appeared the moment
# something was being a field that shifts every name on the screen sideways. A
# format written without "m" simply shows no marks — the reader's title still
# says whether the message in front of you is one, and the marks are made either
# way.
#
# So the default narrow row puts the number, its mark, the two names and the day
# on one line and the subject across the whole of the next, a window that narrow
# having no columns to put them side by side; the wide one is the single row the
# list has always drawn, with the minute beside the day. Both write the stamp out
# rather than leaving it to reader_datetime_format: a column of stamps is read
# down for which day a message is from, and a format with no %z in it measures
# the same from one screenful to the next, where the reader's own would widen and
# narrow as the zones in the messages on screen came and went. The subject is drawn
# in the theme's "dimmed" color wherever it stands. Where the narrow and wide
# formats are not the same height, dragging the window across adaptive_ui_threshold
# keeps the selected message where it stands on the screen.
msglist_format "amf0 t0 d(%d %b %y)\ns" "amf t s d(%d %b %y %H:%M)"

# Whether the message list draws the reader's scrollbar in its rightmost column,
# on the same terms as arealist_scrollbar. Optional; on by default.
msglist_scrollbar on

# What Enter does to a number typed into the message list's goto field, where
# any digit turns the title's msg/total into a box to type a message number into.
# Optional; on by default, and then the message is opened, the field being the
# quick way to it. Off puts the cursor on that row and leaves the list up, for
# looking around the number before opening anything — the row is then opened by
# Enter on it, like any other.
msglist_goto_field_opens on

# Whether a message user hasn't read yet is drawn in the theme's msglist_unread
# color across the whole row. Optional; on by default. A From or To naming you
# keeps the own_name color. What counts as unread is the mark the base itself
# keeps on each message — TimesRead in JAM and in a Fido *.msg, the MSGSEEN bit
# in Squish — and not the unread count in the area list, which is a position:
# a message read out of order is read wherever the mark stands. The marks are
# made either way; this only says whether the list paints them.
highlight_unread on

# --- message reader -----------------------------------------------------------

# Whether the message reader starts with the scrollbar beside the text shown.
# Optional; on by default. The `b` key toggles it for the session.
reader_scrollbar on

# How wide the window has to be before the reader puts the list of messages up
# one side of itself, separated from the message by a vertical rule. Optional; 0
# by default — no panel at any width — and "off" says the same. 81 to 255
# otherwise; 120 is the width worth turning it on at, leaving the message the 80
# columns an FTN message is written to.
#reader_sidebar_threshold 120

# How many columns that panel stands in, the rule beside it not counted.
# Optional; 39 by default, which leaves the message its 80 columns in a window of
# 120; 16 to 255. A fixed strip: every column of a wider window goes to the
# message. A width the window has no room beside leaves the panel off in that
# window rather than being cut down to fit.
reader_sidebar_width 39

# Which side of the message that panel stands on: left or right. Optional; right
# by default, which leaves the message beginning in the window's first column.
# The rule between the two moves with the panel; nothing else about it changes.
reader_sidebar_position right

# What that panel shows: tree or list. Optional; tree by default.
#
# tree is the thread around the message being read, drawn as one: the message
# it answers above it, that message's other answers beside it, and the answers
# to each of those under them. A message answering nothing stands at the top
# with its own answers below.
#
# list is the messages of the area, in the area's own order, with the message
# being read marked in it — the message list standing beside the message rather
# than behind it.
reader_sidebar_content tree

# How many levels of the thread the tree is drawn from above the message being
# read, and how far below it goes. Optional; 1 each by default, 0 to 9.
#
# One each is the message in its own place: what it answers above it, what else
# answers that beside it, and what answers any of those below. 0 above stands
# the tree on the message itself — no message answered above it and none of its
# siblings; 0 below stops at the row the message is on. 0 for both is the
# message alone.
#
# Levels of the thread rather than rows of the panel: everything that many
# levels up reaches is drawn, cut off that many levels under the message. A
# thread shorter than the climb is drawn from its own top, and the levels below
# are counted from the message either way — a message with nothing above it has
# the same depth drawn beneath it as one buried in a thread.
#
# Both are read only under reader_sidebar_content tree.
reader_sidebar_tree_levels_up 1
reader_sidebar_tree_levels_down 1

# What each row of that panel holds. Optional. Written exactly as msglist_format
# is — the same letters, widths and d(...) — but one format rather than two, the
# panel being only ever on the screen in a wide window. The default puts the two
# names and the stamp on one line and the subject on the next; no number column,
# which the reader's own title carries, and so no "m" beside one — write one in
# to have the marks in the panel too.
reader_sidebar_msglist_format "f0 t0 d(%d %b %H:%M)\ns"

# Whether links in the message text are underlined as well as colored.
# Optional; on by default.
reader_underline_links on

# The program a click on a link in the message text opens it with: the command
# and its arguments, with $url standing where the link goes. Optional, and not
# set by default — with no handler named, clicking a link does nothing at all.
#
# The link is written wherever $url appears, in as many arguments as name it, and
# the program is run directly rather than through a shell: it is looked for on
# $PATH the way a name typed at a prompt is, an argument with a space in it is
# written in double quotes, and nothing in a link needs escaping, since there is
# no shell to escape it for. AmberEdit hands the terminal over for as long as the
# program runs and takes it back when it ends, so a text browser has the screen
# and a program that opens a window elsewhere is gone before the screen is
# missed. `xdg-open $url` is the other command worth naming here — it hands the
# link to whatever the desktop opens links with.
##urlhandler lynx $url

# The external utilities: up to ten programs, extern_util0 through extern_util9,
# each written as a title and then the command to run it by. Optional, and none
# are set by default.
#
#   extern_util0 "Scan"  /usr/local/bin/hpt scan
#   extern_util1 "Files" mc
#   extern_util2 "Browser" lynx
#
# The title comes first and is always written: it is the word a menu button and
# a hint are drawn with, and nothing else in the line could be read as one. What
# follows it is the program and its arguments — run directly rather than through
# a shell, so the program is looked for on $PATH the way a name typed at a
# prompt is, an argument with a space in it is written in double quotes, and
# nothing goes near a shell to be escaped for one.
#
# AmberEdit hands the terminal over for as long as the utility runs and takes it
# back when it ends, exactly as it does for your own shell behind reader.shell.
# What the utility did while it had the screen is between it and you: one that
# ran and exited non-zero is not reported.
#
# Nothing runs them until something names them. A utility is reached three ways,
# and every one of them is written down by hand:
#
#   * a key, in the "keys" file, on the area list, in the reader or in the
#     editor — "Alt-F1 reader.extern_util0", "Alt-F2 compose.extern_util1",
#     "Alt-F3 arealist.extern_util2". The slot is the digit and nothing else, so
#     those three lines are three keys and three different utilities;
#   * a button in a context menu — "extern_util0" in reader_menu or in
#     compose_menu, drawn under the ⚒ glyph every utility carries;
#   * a hint — "extern_util0" in arealist_hints, reader_hints or compose_hints,
#     which shows the title under whichever key runs it.
#
# A menu or a hint naming a slot no extern_utilN line sets, and a key bound to
# one, are both refused as AmberEdit starts: a button with nothing behind it is
# a mistake nobody would ever find on the screen.

# Whether coming back from a program that had the terminal — one of the
# utilities above, or your own shell behind reader.shell — also reads every
# other area again, the way Ctrl-R does on the area list. Optional; off by
# default.
#
# The area you are reading is re-read either way, and that is not optional: a
# program with the terminal may have changed the message in front of you,
# delivered into the area, deleted from it or packed it, and every message base
# reads its index once when the area is opened. So AmberEdit opens that one area
# again and puts you back on the message you were reading — found by the
# identifier that outlives a pack, so a packed area leaves you on the same
# message and not on whatever took its number. A message that has since been
# deleted leaves you on the nearest one before it, exactly as a lastread mark on
# a deleted message does. Where you had scrolled to stays where you left it.
#
# What this setting adds is every *other* area's counts, which is what you want
# after a utility that runs your tosser. It costs one open per area and puts the
# same modal on the screen that Ctrl-R does, so on a long area list it is a
# noticeable pause after every utility — which is why it is asked for rather
# than assumed.
#
# The link handler is deliberately not covered by either half: a browser does
# not write to a message base.
rescan_on_return off

# Whether the reader acts on the style codes a message may be written with:
# _underlined_, *bold*, /italic/ and #inverted# phrases. Optional; off by
# default; may be set per area in a group block. A marker only counts where a
# word could start or end, so `snake_case`, `2*3*4`, /usr/local/bin and
# `#include <stdio.h>` are left alone. Italic needs a terminal that draws it.
reader_stylecodes off

# What the reader does at the ends of an area: → on the last message and ← on the
# first. Optional; "exit" by default.
#
#   exit                     back to the area list
#   exit_set_to_next_unread  back to the area list, with the cursor already on
#                            the next area with something unread in it. Where
#                            there are no unread areas, on the next area on the
#                            list. The area is not opened automatically
#   stay                     nothing at all: the two keys stop at the ends
#   next_unread_area         → goes on directly to the next area with something
#                            unread in it. Where there are no unread areas, it
#                            just goes directly to the next area
#   next_unread_only         → goes on directly to the next area with something
#                            unread in it. Where there are no unread areas, it
#                            goes back to the area list
#
#
# Esc leaves the area from anywhere whatever this says, and leaves the mark on
# the message on the screen. An area holding no messages is left by both keys.
reader_edge exit

# Where entering an area from the area list lands: on the message after the
# lastread mark, or on the marked one itself. Optional; on by default. A mark on
# the newest message, and an area with no mark at all, open the same way
# whichever way this stands.
reader_lastread_auto_next on

# Whether an answer follows the "AREA:" line in the beginning of the message
# replying to the area it was posted to.
# Optional; on by default; may be set per area in a group block.
areareplydirect on

# Where the answers to this area's messages belong. One area tag, matched
# whatever its case is written in. Optional; unset by default; may be set per
# area in a group block, which is how one echo says it and the rest do not.
#
# It says two things, and both are about answering:
#
#   * the "reply in another area" dialog — the reader's "n" — opens with its
#     cursor on that area. Only where the cursor starts: nothing is picked for
#     you, and "q" and the Reply button are untouched — they answer in the area
#     on screen, or the one the message's own "AREA:" line names. A tag no area
#     in the list carries opens the dialog at the top of the list, as naming
#     none does;
#   * where the area named is a NETMAIL one, a "CC:" line in a message written
#     in this echo is carried out, and the copies are written there. An echo
#     naming no netmail area has nowhere to put a message addressed to one
#     person, and its "CC:" lines stay in the message as the text you typed.
#reply_to_area NETMAIL

# --- carbon copies and crossposts ---------------------------------------------
#
# A message you write may carry two commands in its own text, GoldED's way and
# in GoldED's spelling. Each has to BEGIN its line, and neither is read in a
# quoted line, in a control line, or in the tearline and origin closing the
# message:
#
#   CC: Ivan Ivanov, 2:5020/1234, #Vasya Pupkin, @friends.lst
#   XC: ru.* ru.linux, #ru.talk
#   XP: ru.linux                          (XP: and XC: are the same command)
#
# "CC:" sends a copy of the message to everybody it names, separated by commas
# alone — a name holds spaces and does not hold a comma. A recipient may be a
# name the nodelist knows, an address ("2:5020/1234", or "5020/1234", "/1234",
# ".5" for what this area's own AKA can finish), a name and an address together,
# or an address_macro. "@file" reads the recipients from a file of names,
# separated by commas and by lines, and a "@" inside that file is passed over.
#
# "XC:"/"XP:" posts the message in every echo its masks name, separated by
# commas, spaces or tabs, with "*" and "?" matching an echotag as they do
# everywhere else. Netmail areas are never crossposted to, an echo named twice
# is written to once, and a mask covering the echo you are writing in leaves it
# where it already is.
#
# A "#" in front of a recipient or a mask carries the copy out and leaves that
# name out of the list the message keeps. On the mask covering the echo you are
# writing in, it also leaves the "Originally in" line unsaid.
#
# Both are carried out when you store the message, and you are asked first.
# Whatever nobody could find — a name no nodelist answers for, a mask no echo
# matches, a file that cannot be read — leaves its line in the message, and a
# box afterwards says what was not done.

# What the message keeps where its "CC:" lines stood. Optional; "names" by
# default; may be set per area in a group block.
#
#   keep      the "CC:" lines stay exactly as you typed them
#   names     "* Carbon copied to Ivan Ivanov, Vasya Pupkin", wrapped at
#             quote_margin
#   visible   "* Carbon copied to Ivan Ivanov  2:5020/1234", one per recipient
#   hidden    a "CC: <name> <address>" kludge per recipient, which a person
#             reading the message does not see
#   remove    the lines go and nothing stands in their place
#
# The list stands where the first "CC:" line it was made from stood.
compose_cc_list names

# And the same for the "XC:"/"XP:" lines. Optional; "verbose" by default; may be
# set per area in a group block. The words are the ones GoldED writes them with:
#
#   raw       the "XC:" lines stay exactly as you typed them
#   verbose   "* Originally in ru.talk" and "* Crossposted in ru.linux, ru.unix",
#             wrapped at quote_margin
#   yes       the same, one "* Crossposted in" line per echo
#   none      the lines go and nothing stands in their place
compose_xc_list verbose

# --- layout configuration -----------------------------------------------------

# The width, in columns, where a window stops counting as a narrow one — the line
# every "when_narrow" and "when_wide" setting is read against. Optional; 80 by
# default, 40 to 255. Under it a window is narrow, at it and over it is wide,
# measured again on every frame. The message list reads the same line without a
# setting of its own: a narrow window drops its To column and gives the room to
# the subjects.
adaptive_ui_threshold 80

# Whether the last row of the screen is the hint bar — the commands of whichever
# screen is up, each behind the key that runs it: "/ next unread  ctrl-r rescan"
# on the area list. Optional; "on" by default, the same four values as
# back_button below and the same line "when_narrow" and "when_wide" are read
# against. A window too narrow for the whole row carries as many hints as fit
# whole and leaves the rest off the end of it, so "when_wide" is the way to ask
# for the row whole or not at all.
#
# The keys come from the layout, so a command the "keys" file leaves unbound is
# left out of the row, and one with several keys is shown under the shortest of
# them — a bare key before a chord, a chord before a function key. A key is
# written in the case the row is — hint_bar_capitalize, below — and the word
# beside it is the one the menu writes on a button for the same command. The row
# is taken off the screen whether or not there is anything to put in it, so
# moving between screens moves nothing else.
#
# Clicking a hint presses the key it is written under, and the hint lights up
# for the length of the click the way every other button does. Standing there it
# looks like what it is — a reminder rather than a row of buttons.
#
# The hints are drawn in the theme's "hint_bar" — dark grey in both themes that
# ship here — and what is left of the row beside them is a rule in "separator",
# the same rule that closes the headings at the top of a screen.
hint_bar on

# Where in the row the hints stand — the rest of it is the rule that closes the
# interface at the bottom, and this is which side of them it runs along.
# Optional; "center" by default.
#   left     / next unread  ctrl-r rescan ──────────
#   center   ───── / next unread  ctrl-r rescan ─────
#   right    ────────── / next unread  ctrl-r rescan
# One setting for every screen's row: the four are one row that changes with the
# screen, and hints that jumped from one side to the other between two screens
# would read as something having moved.
hint_bar_align center

# Whether the row is written in capitals. Optional; off by default, the row being
# a quiet one:
#   on    Q Reply  Ctrl-F Find  F7 Export
#   off   q reply  ctrl-f find  f7 export
# The keys go with the words, which is what makes it one setting rather than two.
# It is the row's own spelling of a key and not the layout's: "g" and "G" are two
# keys in a "keys" file and one hint either way here.
hint_bar_capitalize off

# What each screen's row holds, in the order the hints are to stand. Optional;
# the four lines at the end of this block are the defaults. Each key names
# commands of its own screen, by the part of the name after the dot — the
# "reply_elsewhere" of "reader.reply_elsewhere", which is the spelling the "keys"
# file uses — and "quit" besides, which every screen answers. A "-" in a name
# reads as a "_", so a config written when they were spelled "reply-elsewhere"
# is read as it stands:
#
#   arealist_hints  next_unread  toggle_unread  rescan  quit
#
#   msglist_hints   mark_toggle  quit
#
#   reader_hints    reply  reply_elsewhere  comment_reply  new  forward  change
#                   delete  export  find  list  info  nodelist  kludges
#                   scrollbar  mark_toggle  mark_menu  shell  thread_up
#                   thread_down  quit
#
#   compose_hints   save  attributes  import  header_back  delete_line
#                   restore_line  delete_quote  delete_word  word_left
#                   word_right  line_start  line_end  quit
#
# The area list, the reader and the editor each name "extern_util0" through
# "extern_util9" besides — see extern_utilN above. A hint for one shows the
# title that line gave it.
#
# What each of them does is written beside its key in amberkeys.cfg.example.
#
# "none" on its own is a row with nothing in it, which is what the message list
# is given: every key on it either moves the cursor or marks the message under
# it, and neither is worth a row of the screen. A list may otherwise be as long
# as you like — what the window has no room for is left off the end of the row.
arealist_hints next_unread toggle_unread rescan
msglist_hints none
reader_hints reply reply_elsewhere new list export nodelist
compose_hints save delete_line import

# Whether the reader and the message list show a back button — a left arrow in
# the top-left corner. Optional; "when_narrow" by default. Esc does the same
# whether the button is there or not.
#   on           always, whatever the window is
#   off          never — the title takes the corner back
#   when_narrow  only where the window is narrower than adaptive_ui_threshold
#   when_wide    the other way round: only at that width and over
back_button when_narrow

# Whether the editor shows a delete-line button — a box around the line the
# cursor is on, with a cross in it, drawn down the three rightmost columns and
# over the scrollbar where there is one. Clicking it deletes that line, which is
# what ^Y does from the keyboard. Optional; "when_narrow" by default, the same
# four values as back_button and the same line they are read against.
#
# It costs the message three columns on every row and not only on the row it
# stands beside: the button walks the message with the cursor, and text laid out
# to the full width elsewhere would rewrap under it at every keystroke.
compose_delete_line_button when_narrow

# The context menu: a column of buttons, one command each, opened by the button
# in the top-right corner of the area list, of the reader and of the editor.
# Every command is one a key does too; a button whose command has nothing to do
# is drawn quietly, in the theme's "dialog_hint" color as everything else a box
# shows but will not act on is. The value is the commands in the order they are
# to stand, named the way the hint lists name them — the part after the dot —
# and each screen has its own key and its own commands:
#
#   arealist_menu rescan           read the tosser config and every base again,
#                                  as ^R does
#                 toggle_unread    between the whole list and the areas with
#                                  something unread in them, as ^U does
#                 next_unread      down to the next area with something unread
#                                  in it, as `/` does
#
#   reader_menu   list             the list of messages in the area
#                 reply            answer the message, quoting it
#                 reply_elsewhere  answer it in another area
#                 comment_reply    answer it addressed to whoever it was
#                                  written to, as Alt-Q does
#                 new              write a new message
#                 forward          forward / copy /move the message to another area,
#                                  as `m` does
#                 find             look for a message in the area, as ^F does
#                 change           change the message, as `c`/F2 does
#                 info             what the base holds about it, as `i` does
#                 export           write it out to a text file, as `w` does
#                 nodelist         look an address or a sysop up, as Ctrl-N does
#                 mark_toggle      mark the message, or take the mark off it,
#                                  as `t` does
#                 mark_menu        the box that marks a run of messages at once,
#                                  as `s` does
#                 shell            hand the terminal to your own shell until you
#                                  leave it, as `o` does
#
#   compose_menu  save             store the message being written, as ^S does
#                 import           read a file into the message, as ^O does
#
# All three lists may name "extern_util0" through "extern_util9" as well — see
# extern_utilN above. Such a button is drawn under the ⚒ glyph, with the title
# that line gave the utility beside it.
#
# A menu holds what a button can stand for, which is less than a hint bar may
# name: the rest of what the editor does is editing, and there is no button for
# taking a line out. "none" on its own leaves the screen without a menu, and so
# does menu_button off — the first for one screen, the second for all three.
#
# The three lines below are the defaults. "change", "info", "export",
# "comment_reply", "mark_toggle" and "mark_menu" are left out of the reader's,
# and "next_unread" out of the list's — write them in to have them:
#
#   reader_menu   list reply reply_elsewhere comment_reply new forward find
#                 change info export nodelist mark_toggle mark_menu shell
#
#   arealist_menu rescan toggle_unread next_unread
#
# The message list carries no menu button and has no setting for one: marking
# the message under the cursor is its one command, and one button is no menu.
arealist_menu rescan toggle_unread
reader_menu list reply reply_elsewhere new forward find nodelist shell
compose_menu save import

# Whether the area list, the reader and the editor show the menu button at all.
# Optional; "when_narrow" by default, the same four values as back_button and the
# same line they are read against. On the list it stands over the column headings
# and the rule under them, which are there either way, so it costs no row.
menu_button when_narrow

# How wide one button of the menu stands, in columns, the frame included.
# Optional; 22 by default, 4 to 100 — which is the longest label any of the menus
# offers, glyph and all, with a column or two after it. A label with no room
# left for it is cut, as every other label in the interface is.
menu_buttons_width 22

# Whether a button in a dialog stands in a frame, three rows tall instead of one.
# Optional; "when_narrow" by default, the same four values as back_button and the
# same line they are read against.
#   on           always, whatever the window is
#   off          never — a button is a word with a space either side of it
#   when_narrow  only where the window is narrower than adaptive_ui_threshold
#   when_wide    the other way round: only at that width and over
#
# A one-row button is a target one row tall, and a narrow window is the window
# being pointed at with a finger rather than with a mouse. The button is the same
# width either way: the frame takes the two columns the wider padding takes
# without it, so nothing shifts sideways as a window is dragged past the line.
#
# This doesn't affect the context menu — its buttons are framed whatever this
# says, a column of frames that meet being what makes it read as a list — and
# neither is the setup wizard, which is on the screen before there is a config.
dialog_tall_buttons when_narrow

# How long a click is shown before it acts, in milliseconds. Optional; 100 by
# default, 0 turning the animation off. A click on a button draws it in the
# theme's animated_button_text color for that long — dialog_flash where the
# button stands inside a dialog; a click in a list shows the row it landed on as
# the current one and only then opens it.
click_animation_ms 100

# Whether the wheel is counted against the height of a row in the lists.
# Optional; on by default. A notch of the wheel is a line, so a list whose rows
# stand two or three lines tall — any arealist_format or msglist_format with a
# \n in it — moves two or three times as far under the same flick as anything
# else on the screen.
# With this on, the first notch of a run moves the cursor and the rest of that
# row's worth are swallowed: a two-line row costs two notches and a three-line
# row three. A format written on one line is untouched either way, there being
# nothing to count, which is why this is one setting rather than one per list.
list_wheel_throttle on

# How far apart two notches may be and still count as one run of the wheel, in
# milliseconds. Optional; 200 by default, 0 to 2000. Anything slower than this
# is scrolling by hand rather than a flick, and every notch of it moves the
# cursor: what the counting is for is making a fast wheel proportionate, not
# making a deliberate one wait. 0 turns the counting off as surely as
# list_wheel_throttle off does, there being no run left for a notch to belong
# to.
list_wheel_throttle_ms 200

# For how long the tail of a flick of the wheel is kept off what has just come
# up in front of the user, in milliseconds. Optional; 1500 by default, 0 to
# 5000. A notch arrives after the hand that asked for it — a trackpad goes on
# reporting them once the finger has left it — so the ones still coming when Esc
# closes the reader, when a box opens over it or when the right arrow walks to
# the next message were aimed at what has just been put away, and would scroll
# what took its place instead. They are swallowed while they keep arriving, and
# this is how long that may go on for; past it a hand that keeps turning the
# wheel gets what is now in front of it. A flick begun inside that time is lost
# with the tail — nothing the terminal reports tells the two apart — so raise
# this only as far as the tails you actually see. 0 turns it off, and the tail
# lands where it falls.
wheel_settle_ms 1500

# --- dates --------------------------------------------------------------------

# How every stamp shown while reading is written: the two in the reader's header
# block (Date, and Recd under it), the one beside each answer in the replies
# dialog, the file stamps in the import dialog, and the one at the head of an
# exported message. Optional; "%d %b %y %H:%M %z" by default. One setting for the
# date and the time both, because each place showing them shows one string.
#
# The message list's Date column is written with this too, and is the one place
# that may say otherwise: msglist_format's d(...) gives that column a format of
# its own, which is what both of its defaults do.
#
# The value is a strftime(3) format: %d day, %m month, %b month by name, %y and
# %Y the year in two digits and in four, %H:%M:%S the time, %a the weekday, %% a
# literal "%". AmberEdit sets no LC_TIME, so %a and %b come out in English.
#
# %z writes the offset from UTC the message's own TZUTC control line states
# ("+0300", "-0330") and writes nothing where the message carries no TZUTC — an
# FTN stamp is in no time zone of its own, and nothing is worked out from this
# machine's zone. The arrival stamp is passed no zone at all. The stamp is
# trimmed at both ends, so the space before a %z that wrote nothing goes with it.
#
# Every column showing a stamp is measured, so a format may be as long as it
# likes; what it may not do is write more than one line (%n, %t) or nothing but
# blank.
reader_datetime_format "%d %b %y %H:%M %z"

# Whether the header block carries a "Recd" row — when the message arrived here —
# under the Date row saying when it was written. Optional; "off" by default; the
# same four values as back_button. Drawn exactly as the Date row is. The same row
# in the reader and in the editor, so the two blocks go on lining up field for
# field; a message written here arrived from nowhere and the row is simply blank
# rather than coming and going with the message.
show_recd_date off

# What the message template's date and time tokens are written with: @cdate and
# @ctime for the message being written, @odate and @otime for the one being
# answered. Optional; "%d %b %y" and "%H:%M" by default; strftime formats like
# the one above, and both may be set per area in a group block. Two settings and
# not one, because a template writes them through two tokens.
#
# %z writes the sender's time zone
template_date_format "%d %b %y"
template_time_format "%H:%M"

# --- writing a message --------------------------------------------------------

# Theme file to draw the interface with. Optional: without it AmberEdit uses its
# built-in palette, which themes/black.cfg is a copy of.
#theme /usr/share/amberedit/themes/black.cfg         # the built-in palette
#theme /usr/share/amberedit/themes/blue.cfg          # a navy screen
#theme /usr/share/amberedit/themes/white.cfg         # paper, for a light terminal
#theme /usr/share/amberedit/themes/16_colors.cfg     # sixteen-color DOS look

# The interface's own language is the environment's, as it is for every other
# program on the system: LANGUAGE, LC_ALL, LC_MESSAGES, LANG, in that order of
# preference. `LANG=ru_RU.UTF-8 amberedit` is Russian, and there is no setting
# here for it — where the catalogs live is compiled in.
#
# One thing is the system's rather than AmberEdit's: the locale you ask for has
# to exist. A stock Debian or Ubuntu generates none at all, and gettext cannot
# translate under `C` — `apt install locales && locale-gen ru_RU.UTF-8` settles
# it, and `locale -a` lists what a system already has. AmberEdit says so on
# startup when the locale you asked for is not there, and runs in English.

# The editor a message is written in: the command and its arguments, with $msg
# standing where the file holding the message goes. Optional, and not set by
# default — with none named, messages are written in AmberEdit's own editor.
#
#   external_editor mcedit $msg
#   external_editor vim -c "set tw=72" $msg
#   external_editor emacs -nw $msg
#
# The file is written wherever $msg appears, in as many arguments as name it,
# and the program is run directly rather than through a shell: it is looked for
# on $PATH the way a name typed at a prompt is, an argument with a space in it
# is written in double quotes, and nothing needs escaping since there is no
# shell to escape it for. AmberEdit hands the terminal over for as long as the
# editor runs and takes it back when it ends.
#
# How a message is written with one named:
#
#   * a new message and a forward ask their header first, exactly as they always
#     did. Enter off the subject, Tab off the last stop, or a click in the
#     message below the block hands it over — the text you would have been given
#     to type into is what the editor opens on, template and all;
#   * a reply, a comment reply, a reply into another area and a message being
#     changed go straight to the editor: their headers are filled in already;
#   * the file is written in the character set your terminal uses, under tmpdir,
#     and is taken away again when the message is stored or dropped. Nothing is
#     left behind.
#
# What happens when the editor ends is decided by the file:
#
#   * left without writing — not one byte different — is how you say you did not
#     want the message, PROVIDED you have not been asked the question below yet.
#     Nothing is stored and the reader comes back. Once that question has been
#     on the screen, going back into the editor and changing nothing simply
#     brings it back — Discard is on it already, and that is where throwing the
#     message away belongs;
#   * written, and AmberEdit shows what you wrote under the header block and
#     asks: Save it, Discard it, Continue editing (the same editor on the same
#     file again), or edit the Header. Esc is the last of those — it neither
#     stores nor throws anything away.
#
# The message is SHOWN and never typed into while an editor is named: there is
# no half-and-half, and the commands that edit text — reading a file in above
# all — do nothing here. The header block works exactly as it always did.
#external_editor mcedit $msg

# The GoldED-style message template. Required and has to be readable; may be set
# per area in a group block.
template /usr/share/amberedit/default.tpl

# Skip template for the robots (new netmails only)
# Default: AreaFix AreaMgr AllFix FileFix T-Fix FaqServer
#netmail_skip_template AreaFix AreaMgr AllFix FileFix T-Fix FaqServer

# What a quoted line is prefixed with. Optional; " FL> " by default, which is
# GoldED's — the letters before the ">" stand for the initials of whoever is
# being quoted. Exactly one ">" is required: that character is what quote levels
# are counted in, so a second would send a first-level quote out looking like a
# second-level one.
quote_string " FL> "

# The column quoted text is wrapped at. Optional; 78 by default, which keeps a
# line inside the 79 characters FTN messages are written to and leaves one in
# hand for the next quote level; 20 to 255. It says nothing about the lines you
# type yourself, which are wrapped by whatever shows them.
quote_margin 78

# Whether a paragraph is put back together before it is quoted. Optional; off by
# default; may be set per area in a group block.
#
# Off, a quoted line carries the break the editor the message was written in put
# there, so answering a message written to a wider margin leaves a quote of two
# words to the line. On, the lines a paragraph was wrapped into are joined and
# the text is wrapped again at quote_margin.
#
# The lines joined are the ones standing together at one quote level under the
# same initials, where the line above had no room left for the next line's first
# word. A blank line, a deeper quote, another writer's initials, anything
# indented or spaced into columns, and anything beginning a bullet or a numbered
# point all end a paragraph: those breaks are the writer's, and a table, a
# signature or a piece of ASCII art is quoted exactly as it stands either way.
quote_unwrap off

# The two lines standing either side of a file read into a message as text — ^O
# in the editor, and "import" in compose_menu. Optional; "=== Cut ===" both. An
# empty one writes no line at all. Only text is fenced: a file read in as UUE
# carries its own "begin" and "end".
import_begin "=== Cut ==="
import_end "=== Cut ==="

# The two lines every message is signed with. "tearline" is what stands after the
# "--- " that closes the text; "origin" is what stands between " * Origin: " and
# the address in brackets under it. Both are single lines with the same
# replacement tokens a template uses, so "@longpid @version" — the default
# tearline — names AmberEdit and its version without the version being written
# here (@longpid is the name with the system under it, @pid the bare name).
#
# The origin text is empty by default: it is your own words about your system.
# The line itself is written whatever it says — an echomail message without one
# is one a tosser may refuse — and an empty tearline goes out as "---" alone.
#
# Either may name a file of them instead — "origin @file:origins.txt" — and then
# each message you write is signed with one of its lines, picked at random.
tearline "@longpid @version"
origin "Somewhere in the world"

# --- twits --------------------------------------------------------------------

# Whom you would rather not read. Optional; as many lines of either as you like,
# and a message matching ANY of them is a twit.
#
#   twit "Ivan Ivanov"        that name, whoever holds it
#   twit *Spammer*            every name holding "Spammer"
#   twit 2:9999/*             everything written from net 9999 of zone 2
#   twit_subj "*for sale*"    every message about that, whoever wrote it
#
# A "twit" line names either a name or an FTN address, and which of the two it is
# is settled by whether it parses as an address holding a zone. A NAME is matched
# whole, with "*" for any run of characters and "?" for exactly one, case folded
# for ASCII — so a rule to be found *inside* a name says so with stars of its
# own. An ADDRESS is the pattern the akamatch lines are written in. A message
# carrying no address — which is every echomail message in a JAM base, the format
# keeping none there — is not covered by a rule about addresses. "twit_subj" is
# the same kind of glob over the subject line.
#
# A long list is better kept in a file: "twit @file:twit.list" adds every line of
# that file to the ones written here, and "twit_subj @file:twit_subj.list" does
# the same for the subjects.
#twit "Ivan Ivanov"
#twit_subj "*for sale*"

# Whether a "twit" line covers the messages written TO that name or address as
# well as the ones written from it. Optional; on by default. It says nothing
# about twit_subj: a subject has no direction to have an opinion about.
twit_to on

# What the reader does with a twit. Optional; "blank" by default.
#
#   show      nothing at all: the lines are written and the messages stand
#   blank     the text replaced by "This is a twit message. Press Space to view
#             it", and Space shows it after all
#   skip      walked past, unless the message is addressed to you
#   ignore    walked past whatever it says
#   kill      DELETED from the message base, without asking, as the area opens
#
# "skip" and "ignore" walk in the direction you are reading, and a message picked
# out of the list — or the one an area opens on — is treated as a place in the
# area the same way. A run of twits at the end of the area is the end of it,
# which is where reader_edge has its say; where every message in an area is
# a twit there is nowhere to skip to and the area reads as "blank" reads.
#
# "kill" cannot be undone: every twit is taken out of the area as it is opened —
# not marked, not hidden, deleted — and no confirmation is asked for. This
# setting is the confirmation, given once here.
twit_mode blank

# --- values kept in a file ------------------------------------------------------
#
# Four settings may name a file to take their values from instead of writing
# them out: "origin", "tearline", "twit" and "twit_subj". Write "@file:" and the
# file's name after the key, and nothing else on the line.
#
#   origin    @file:origins.txt         a different origin on each message
#   tearline  @file:tearlines.txt       and a different tearline
#   twit      @file:/etc/ftn/twit.list  everyone in the file, ignored
#   twit_subj @file:twit_subj.list      every subject in it, ignored
#
# A name without a directory is looked for beside this config; a name with one is
# taken as it stands. A name with spaces in it goes in quotes like any other
# value — origin "@file:my origins.txt".
#
# The file holds ONE VALUE PER LINE. A line is taken whole: nothing is split on
# spaces, no quotes are taken off, and a "#" or a quote inside a line is text.
# Blank lines are skipped, and so are lines a "#" opens — that being the only
# comment such a file has.
#
# The two lines a message is signed with take ONE of the values, picked at random
# each time you write a message: that is what a file of origins is for. The two
# twit lists take ALL of them, added to whatever the config wrote out itself.
#
# The files are read once, when AmberEdit starts, so a file you edit while it is
# running is picked up the next time it starts. One that cannot be read stops
# AmberEdit there and then, naming the line: a twit list silently holding nobody
# would be a config quietly reading everybody.
#
# Nowhere else does "@file:" mean anything — a "template" or a "name" beginning
# with it is that text. (A "CC:" or an "XC:" line inside a message has a "@file"
# of its own, spelled without the colon; see "carbon copies and crossposts".)

# --- area groups --------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Everything above is the answer for every area you read. An area group is the
# answer for some of them: a "group ... endgroup" block naming the areas it
# covers on "member" lines, and restating the settings those areas are read and
# written under.
#
#   group
#     member esp.*
#     default_charset LATIN-1
#     compose_charset LATIN-1
#   endgroup
#
#   group
#     member r50.*
#     member min_admin
#     name Vasiliy Pupkin
#   endgroup
#
# A "member" pattern is matched against the area tag and nothing else, with "*"
# for any run of characters (the empty one included) and "?" for exactly one;
# everything else stands for itself, the dots included, so "esp.*" covers
# esp.argentina and esp.charla.libre alike and "*" covers every area you have.
# Case is folded for ASCII, and a "member" line takes as many patterns as you
# like and may be written as often as you like.
#
# Where two groups both cover an area, the one whose pattern says more about the
# tag wins — the literal characters before its first wildcard, then the literal
# characters in the whole of it, and a pattern with no wildcard at all beating
# one that has any: "esp.argentina" beats "*.argentina", which beats "esp.*",
# which beats "*". They win setting by setting, so a group over "member *" can
# give every area an origin while a group over "member esp.*" gives some of them
# a charset. Two groups that say exactly as much about one area and both state
# the same setting are refused at startup.
#
# What a group may state, and nothing else — a key not on this list is refused
# rather than quietly ignored:
#
#   name                  address
#   default_charset       compose_charset
#   origin                tearline
#   template              quote_string          quote_margin
#   quote_unwrap
#   template_date_format  template_time_format
#   import_begin          import_end
#   reader_stylecodes     areareplydirect       reply_to_area
#   reply_original_charset
#   bbs_codes_renegade    bbs_codes_ansi
#   compose_cc_list       compose_xc_list
#   twit                  twit_subj             twit_to      twit_mode
#
# A group's "twit" and "twit_subj" lines are ADDED to the ones outside it, and a
# group cannot take a twit off the list — "twit_mode show" is the one thing that
# says "not here". An "address" stated in a group overrides the one the tosser
# config gives that area. Everything else is the whole config's: the layout, the
# menus, the theme, the area list's own order and columns, the tosser config, and
# the aka / akamatch rules.

# --- experimental options -----------------------------------------------------

# What is under here is not meant for the whole of your mail. It's recommended to
# turn these options on in a group block for the echoes that want it and leave it
# off everywhere else:
#
#group
#  member fsx_ads
#  bbs_codes_ansi on
#endgroup
#
#group
#  member fsx_*
#  bbs_codes_renegade on
#endgroup
#
# See "area groups" above for what a group is and how a member pattern is
# matched.

# Whether the reader acts on the BBS color codes — "pipe codes" — in the
# Renegade/Telegard dialect: a pipe and two decimal digits, |00 to |31.
# See also http://wiki.synchro.net/custom:colors#renegade_telegard_format
# Optional; off by default; may be set per area in a group block.
bbs_codes_renegade off

# Whether the reader replays the ANSI graphics a message may have been written
# with — the escape sequences a BBS drew a picture on the caller's terminal
# with. Optional; off by default; may be set per area in a group block.
#
# The setting says an echo may carry ANSI; each message is still looked at on its
# own, and only one that actually holds an escape sequence is drawn this way. The
# rest of the echo reads as it always did.
#
# A message that is drawn this way is replayed onto a canvas 80 columns wide —
# the width the art was composed at — and the canvas is what the reader scrolls.
# Nothing in it wraps: a window narrower than the picture shows the left of it
# and the rest is off screen. Colors, cursor movement, the erasers, and saving
# and restoring the cursor under either spelling — ESC 7 / ESC 8 and ESC[s /
# ESC[u — are acted on. Bold is the bright half of the palette, as it was on the
# adapter these codes were written for, and blinking is passed over.
#
# Inside such a message reader_stylecodes and bbs_codes_renegade do not apply
# however they are set: a marker in a picture is a glyph somebody drew with. The
# tearline and the origin line stay out of the picture and keep the theme's
# trailer color, wherever the drawing left off.
bbs_codes_ansi off
